 ## Operating characteristics
-
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N_{iters} \cdot N_{rand} \cdot N_{samples} \cdot D)$, where $D$ is the network depth/width. Training is computationally intensive due to the large number of iterations (100k–1M) and the need to query the network for every sample point [S11, S14, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N_{rand} \cdot N_{samples} \cdot D)$ for the forward pass, mitigated by chunking. The pre-computed ray array scales with the number of training images and resolution [S5, S14, unofficial].
-*   **Measured Performance:** On 128x128 images, NeRF can be trained for 250 epochs (or ~100k iterations) [S4, S11, unofficial]. Training on 199 down-sampled images (200x200) with batch size 1024 results in ~7,800 iterations per epoch [S17, unofficial].
-*   **Scaling:** Performance scales with batch size, but larger batches hinder data loading performance and prevent full GPU occupancy. Smaller batches cap iterations per second. A batch size of $2^{10}$ (1024) is often chosen as a trade-off [S15, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Simple to implement; leverages standard deep learning frameworks; produces high-quality novel views when given sufficient input images [S5, S14, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Slow convergence; requires many input images; prone to floaters and artifacts in sparse view settings; high memory usage during training [S1][S17, unofficial].
-*   **When to Use:** When high-quality novel view synthesis is required and sufficient multi-view images are available.
-*   **When Not to Use:** For real-time applications without acceleration techniques (e.g., Instant-NGP); for scenes with very sparse input views without specialized initialization [S1][S17, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N_{iters} \cdot N_{rand} \cdot K)$, where $N_{iters}$ is the number of iterations, $N_{rand}$ is the batch size (rays per step), and $K$ is the number of samples per ray [S5, S11, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N_{rand} \cdot K)$ for storing intermediate rendering states per batch, plus model memory [S5, unofficial]. VRAM overflow is a common pitfall, mitigated by chunking [S5, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:** Training typically requires 100,000 iterations [S11, unofficial]. For a 199-image dataset, 5 epochs with batch size 1024 results in ~39,000 iterations [S17, unofficial].
+*   **Scaling:** Larger batch sizes (e.g., $2^{14}$) hinder data loading performance and prevent full GPU occupancy, while smaller batch sizes (e.g., $2^{10}$) cap iterations per second [S15, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** End-to-end differentiable, handles complex geometry and appearance, produces high-quality novel views.
+*   **Limitations:** Slow training convergence, high memory usage, prone to floaters and artifacts in sparse views [S1][S17, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-
-The NeRF Training Loop originates from the seminal paper "NeRF: Representing Scenes as Neural Radiance Fields for View Synthesis" by Mildenhall et al. (2020) [S5, S11, unofficial]. It was developed to address the limitations of traditional 3D reconstruction methods by using a fully differentiable neural network to represent the scene. The method relies on the differentiability of the volume rendering equation, allowing gradients to flow from the image loss back to the network weights [S5, S11, unofficial]. Since its introduction, it has become the standard baseline for inverse volume rendering, with numerous variants optimizing the loop for speed, sparsity, and geometric accuracy [S1][S11, S15, unofficial].
+The NeRF Training Loop was introduced in the seminal paper "NeRF: Representing Scenes as Neural Radiance Fields for View Synthesis" by Mildenhall et al. (2020) [S5, S18, unofficial]. It builds upon the concept of coordinate-based neural networks for implicit representations [S3, unofficial]. The method has since been extended with various optimization strategies, including coarse-fine training [S5, unofficial], adaptive sampling [S22, unofficial], and different rendering models [S19, unofficial][S23]. It is widely used in computer vision and graphics for novel view synthesis, 3D reconstruction, and scene understanding [S1][S18, unofficial].
 
